ROCR-Runtime (pinned commit 2b22ab0195cc1461cd9abf3b969e9dd7c10af350,
therock-7.14 / ROCm 7.14.0): restore legacy (pre-1.0, type 0/1) doorbell
support in the HSA GPU-agent constructor and AQL queue dispatch path, so
Polaris (gfx803, DoorbellType 0 or 1 depending on board) is accepted as a
valid HSA agent instead of rejected at enumeration.

WHY
---
runtime/hsa-runtime/core/runtime/amd_gpu_agent.cpp's GpuAgent constructor
unconditionally throws whenever a node's Capability.DoorbellType != 2:

    if (node_props.Capability.ui32.DoorbellType != 2)
        throw AMD::hsa_exception(HSA_STATUS_ERROR,
            "Agent creation failed.\nThe GPU node uses a deprecated doorbell type\n");

Polaris reports DoorbellType 0 or 1 ("legacy" doorbell, board-dependent),
so this throw is the entire reason ROCm 7 rejects the card at enumeration
-- rocminfo/clinfo see zero devices, confirmed against
https://github.com/ROCm/clr/issues/269 (closed "not planned" by AMD: gfx803
isn't a supported target). See gfx803/EVALUATION_ROCM_7.md, Layer 1, for
the full investigation of what this check does and does not gate.

Removing the throw alone is not sufficient, though that's the fix an
initial read of amd_gpu_agent.cpp alone suggests. The doorbell write path
(AqlQueue::StoreRelaxed, in amd_aql_queue.cpp) unconditionally assumes
DoorbellType 2 semantics: a single hardware pointer write with no
software bookkeeping. Legacy doorbell types need an entirely different
dispatch protocol -- a software-maintained "max dispatched" watermark
under a spinlock, with type-0 boards indexing by AQL-packet-size-scaled
slot and type-1 boards indexing by raw dispatch id -- which upstream
deleted in commit 39ecc88 when DoorbellType 2 became the only supported
value. Confirmed present and necessary by testing: with only the
amd_gpu_agent.cpp throw removed, agent creation succeeds but queue
dispatch either silently drops packets or writes through a null/wrong
pointer, since signal_.hardware_doorbell_ptr is HSA_SIGNAL_KIND_DOORBELL-
only and was never given a legacy counterpart to write through.

This patch is not original investigation by this repo -- it restores,
verbatim, an AMD engineer's (lucbruni-amd) fix, verified on real Polaris
hardware (RX 550) via a from-source TheRock build: "rocminfo and clinfo
both detect gfx803 (RX 550) successfully." See
https://github.com/lucbruni-amd/TheRock/commit/3d4ad6093a0069876fbfff590bb56ddb340ae9c5
(branch lb/gfx803-polaris-support), specifically
patches/gfx803/rocm-systems/0001-rocr-Restore-legacy-doorbell-support-for-gfx8-Polari.patch
within that commit. Reproduced here, re-diffed clean against this repo's
own pinned rocm-systems commit (2b22ab0195, therock-7.14), not just
copied -- `git apply --check` against that exact commit passes with no
fuzz. That patch's own commit message notes the upstream deletion this
restores: https://github.com/ROCm/rocm-systems/commit/39ecc88.

Confirms the EVALUATION_ROCM_7.md Layer 1 finding that the runtime block
"has a small, proven fix" -- but the actual proven fix is a full legacy
doorbell dispatch path restore across 4 files (~76 lines), not the
single guarded exception that first read of amd_gpu_agent.cpp alone
suggests. Getting past enumeration and getting real dispatch working
turn out to be the same fix, not two.

WHAT
----
- amd_gpu_agent.cpp: remove the DoorbellType != 2 throw.
- amd_hsa_signal.h: add a legacy_hardware_doorbell_ptr (uint32_t*) member
  to the amd_signal_s doorbell union, alongside the existing
  hardware_doorbell_ptr (uint64_t*) used by type-2 boards.
- amd_aql_queue.h / amd_aql_queue.cpp: cache the agent's DoorbellType on
  the queue at construction time (doorbell_type_); branch queue-resource
  setup, signal-kind selection, and GetInfo's DOORBELL_ID query on it;
  and give StoreRelaxed a second path for doorbell_type_ != 2 that
  reproduces the deleted software watermark protocol (CAS-guarded
  max-dispatched update, then a type-0/type-1-specific doorbell write).

Applies to: ROCR-Runtime @ rocm-systems commit 2b22ab0195cc1461cd9abf3b969e9dd7c10af350
(therock-7.14 / ROCm 7.14.0 pin). Not gfx803-specific in the sense of
touching arch-gated code -- this is generic HSA dispatch-protocol logic;
gfx803 (and any other pre-1.0-doorbell board, though none of the others
this repo targets report legacy doorbells) is simply the only consumer
of the doorbell_type_ != 2 branch. See
hsa-agent-rejects-legacy-doorbell.sh for how this is applied and
verified at build time.

UPDATE (2026-08-24): missing _mm_sfence() in the legacy StoreRelaxed
branch -- HIP graph-replay hang root-caused to this
----------------------------------------------------------------------
vLLM HIP graph replay hangs indefinitely on this hardware (`replay()`
spins forever in VirtualGPU::WaitForQueueSlot). Root-caused via real
hardware MQD register dump (`/sys/kernel/debug/kfd/mqds`, cross-checked
against `/proc/<pid>/mem` ring contents) to a genuine CP (compute-pipe)
stall: cp_hqd_pq_wptr correctly advances (confirms the queue's software
write-index proxy, cp_hqd_pq_wptr_poll_addr -> max_legacy_doorbell_dispatch_id_plus_1,
is being read correctly -- this patch's queue_rsrc wiring is not at
fault), but cp_hqd_pq_rptr freezes on a slot whose AQL packet header is
still HSA_PACKET_TYPE_INVALID (1) -- the exact value rocclr's
appendPacketToFlatBuffer() stages there before the real header is
released. CP is correctly obeying the AQL protocol (spin-wait until a
slot's header goes valid); the header write from rocclr's
packet_store_release() (rocvirtual.cpp) simply never became visible
before the doorbell woke the CP.

The type-2 doorbell path this patch left untouched calls _mm_sfence()
immediately before its doorbell write, specifically to drain the CPU's
write-combining buffers for the AQL ring memory before the (separately
mapped, confirmed pgprot_noncached/UC via the amdkfd doorbell mmap
handler) doorbell BAR write executes -- UC writes are strongly ordered
against each other but NOT automatically ordered after a still-buffered
WC write from a different mapping without an explicit fence (x86 SDM
11.3.1). The legacy branch added by this patch reproduces every other
part of the deleted watermark protocol but dropped this fence entirely.
On an idle consumer thread (exactly graph replay's pattern: ring the
doorbell, then wait) there is no other memory traffic to force the WC
buffer to drain, so the gap is not a transient delay -- it is a stall
with no natural end, matching the observed permanent hang exactly.

Fix: add the same _mm_sfence() to the legacy branch, before either
memory-order-release store that becomes visible to the GPU (the
wptr-proxy field write and/or the doorbell-pointer write). One line.
NOT YET RE-VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE as of this patch text -- see
tools/rocr-novad-rebuild/ for the standalone ROCR-Runtime rebuild used
to test this without a full image rebuild, and SESSION_HANDOFF.md for
the verification result once run.

